== D.4.7 exponent bookkeeping: sum over depth of the (D.15) bound, constants explicit ==
  (D.15) with p_t <= (24 + o(1)) M D/u gives bits <= 2 pi sqrt(24) / ln 2 * M/sqrt(u) = 44.41 M/sqrt(u) per node; M_d <= C s 8^-d, u_d = floor(eps lambda / 2^d):
  sum_d 8^-d sqrt(2^d) = 1.2147, so total <= 44.41 * 1.2147 / sqrt(eps) * C s / sqrt(lambda) = 19530 C s / sqrt(lambda) bits (plus H = s/sqrt(lambda) for the leaf and poly factors)
  i.e. the proved exponent is O(s/sqrt(log s)) with a constant of order 10^4 C (C >= 4k for the D.2 network),
    k=3, m=4.26n: bound < 2^n needs sqrt(lambda) > 1.23e+06, lambda > 1.52e+12, s > 2^1.52e+12
    k=3, m=1.0n: bound < 2^n needs sqrt(lambda) > 4.69e+05, lambda > 2.2e+11, s > 2^2.2e+11
    k=2, m=1.0n: bound < 2^n needs sqrt(lambda) > 3.12e+05, lambda > 9.76e+10, s > 2^9.76e+10
  (these use only the explicit constants; C and the 'sufficiently large s' thresholds make the true crossover larger)
== Item 4: degree hypothesis for the D.2 network with one variable in every clause ==
  n = 2..12, k = 2..4, m = 3n, x_1 in every clause: max degree before/after subdivision <= max(k,3) (observed max 4); edges <= 4 k s; bipartite. The equality tree (d-2 trivalent vertices) is the splitting step.
  ceil(s^0.9) < 3 only for s in [1, 2]; for n from 10 to 10^6 (s = n + m >= 10) the hypothesis holds with k = 3.
  k = 3: max degree max(k,3) = 3 <= ceil(s^0.9) from s = 3
  k = 5: max degree max(k,3) = 5 <= ceil(s^0.9) from s = 5
  k = 10: max degree max(k,3) = 10 <= ceil(s^0.9) from s = 12
  k = 20: max degree max(k,3) = 20 <= ceil(s^0.9) from s = 27
  3-CNF, m = 4.26 n, all variables with >= 3 occurrences: wires after subdivision = 2(2km - 3n) = 45.12 n = 8.58 s
